      "writtenOn": "2025-12-09T01:26:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually, the format of printing out is independent of how it is stored (either in the EC flash or in the EEPROM) and how it is used.\n\nThe way the UFSC is stored (or the `cbi_get_ufsc()` you mentioned) depends on the common `cbi_get_board_info()` call, which uses byte-ordered little-endian. Since this is a common call that other CBI fields share, we don\u0027t have a strong reason not to use it.\n\nWhen the UFSC is used, it is cast to a `struct cbi_struct`, which is 32-bit-ordered little-endian (or `uint32_t data[4]`). Since all the EC CPUs are 32-bit architecture, this helps speed up the computation, like bit-wise operations.\n\nBack to the question, when the UFSC is printed, it follows the same format as the `ectool cbi`. The format of `ectool cbi` has a historical reason that some other CBI fields, like battery configs, are already decided to use byte-ordered little-endian. So the UFSC field simply follows that decision.",
      "parentUuid": "56e4e26d_4151be27",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 56,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 69
      },
      "revId": "cd49179d899c56c757e17d295e28f842785bcc67",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1ba2d088_6eedf11c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-09T02:31:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`cbi_get_board_info` doesn\u0027t do anything with endianness because it treats values as opaque bytes. Your comment that \"all EC CPUs are 32-bit architecture\" glosses over the assumption that all EC CPUs are little-endian 32-bit which may currently be the case but may not be the case in the future.\n\nSince it\u0027s assumed that the values are little-endian in the EEPROM this change doesn\u0027t seem problematic, but code that interprets the UFSC value as multibyte integers would be wrong on a big-endian CPU (it would need to byte-swap the values first). It also seems misleading to refer to the output as little-endian, since it\u0027s printed as opaque bytes without interpreting the values as the integers they are.",
      "parentUuid": "a1e51804_ab1c945a",
      "range": {
